explanation:
A persistent session is identified by the client ID. When the client connects with Clean Session = false (MQTT 3.1.1), or Clean Start = false plus a session expiry interval (MQTT 5), the broker stores:
• all of the client's subscriptions;
• QoS 1 and 2 messages that arrived while it was offline;
• in-flight QoS 1/2 messages not yet acknowledged.

On reconnection the client picks up where it left off without resubscribing — useful for mobile or intermittently connected devices.

Port 1883 is plain MQTT: the CONNECT packet carries the username and password in clear text, and every PUBLISH payload is readable by anyone on the path.

The fix for eavesdropping is encryption: MQTT over TLS (conventionally port 8883). TLS provides:
• confidentiality — payloads and credentials are encrypted;
• integrity — tampering is detected;
• server authentication — clients can verify they reached the real broker (and with client certificates, mutual authentication).

ACLs (authorization) and QoS (reliability) solve different problems.

Shared credentials create a single point of compromise:
• one captured device exposes the secret for the entire fleet;
• the attacker can impersonate any device, inject false data or commands;
• you cannot revoke one device without re-provisioning all of them;
• logs cannot tell devices apart, so there is no accountability.

The fix: a unique identity per device — ideally an X.509 certificate or per-device key stored in secure hardware — so a breach is contained to one device that can be revoked individually.

IoT telemetry is mostly append-only data points of (device, metric, timestamp, value). Time-series databases are optimised for this:
• very high write throughput;
• indexes organised by time (and tags such as device ID), so "device X between 10:00 and 11:00" is fast;
• strong compression of similar consecutive values;
• retention policies and downsampling (keep raw data 30 days, hourly averages for years);
• built-in time functions: windows, moving averages, rates.

Adding a fog/gateway layer is a classic trade-off:
Benefits:
• lower latency — decisions are made locally;
• lower bandwidth and cloud cost — only aggregated or selected data goes upstream;
• better resilience — local operation continues during Internet outages;
• privacy — raw data can stay on site.
Costs:
• more local infrastructure to buy, deploy, power, secure, patch and monitor;
• a distributed system that is harder to manage than a purely cloud-based one.

