A company has deployed 5,000 IoT controllers at remote sites. During a security investigation, the following weaknesses are discovered:
• all controllers use the same username and password;
• MQTT messages travel across the Internet without encryption;
• authenticated controllers can publish to any MQTT topic;
• firmware updates are downloaded remotely but are installed without verifying their authenticity; and
• a failed firmware update can leave a controller unable to boot.

The engineering manager argues: "The devices already require a username and password, so the system is sufficiently secure."

As the security consultant:

(24a) Critically evaluate the manager's statement by distinguishing authentication from authorization. Apply both concepts directly to the scenario.

